  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 AM EDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n \nIn the upper levels, a ridge will be over the Tennessee and Ohio\nRiver Valleys. A trough remains over New England. A closed low \nover the Southwest is drifting eastward very slowly. At the \nsurface, weak high pressure will be centered near the Carolinas. \nRidging and high pressure through Thursday will bring dry, sunny \nweather along with a warming trend. Expect highs in the 80s for \nthe rest of the workweek (around 90 degrees near Chattanooga). \n\nBy Friday, a ridge will be centered near FL/GA/SC as a trough dips \ninto the Ohio Valley. The position of the ridge and surface high to \nthe southeast will supply deep layer moisture from the Gulf into the \nTennessee Valley. Rain chances start to increase on Friday but \nhigher chances are likely on Saturday as a slow moving cold front \nmoves into the region. This boundary will likely linger over the \nregion through Sunday before a shortwave finally pushes it through \nlate Sunday. Confidence is increasing in heavy rain potential. Rain \ntotals of 2 to 3 inches will be possible this weekend. Flooding \npotential will likely be low as dry as it has been but some minor \nissues cannot be ruled out. \n\nOn Monday, rain chances will likely taper off as the system moves \nout and high pressure moves in from the north. Highs will be cooler \nin the 70s for Monday.",
